# battery-control.capture — the CONTROL capture for `./arroyo battery`'s own judge.
#
# WHAT THIS IS. A short synthetic serial capture that a clean tree would produce: it carries one
# line for every PASS pattern the battery's judged steps assert, and it carries NONE of the four
# shapes that red a step (a fault line, a missing pattern, a truncation verdict, a nonzero rc).
# It is not a boot log and it is never compared against a kernel — it is the known answer that the
# JUDGE is measured against.
#
# WHO READS IT. `battery_control_probe()` in `unaos/arroyo`, once, at the top of `battery()`,
# before any step runs. It feeds this file to `_step` twice:
#   (A) as-is                     — the judge MUST say ✅. A judge that cannot say ✅ scores an hour
#                                   of QEMU and reports red no matter what the kernel did, which is
#                                   exactly what happened to `x86 test-fat sf 300` from BANNERCERT
#                                   to BATTFAT: nothing in `battery()` had ever asked whether a
#                                   step's judge COULD go green.
#   (B) with one fault line added — the judge MUST say ❌ and MUST name `fault line`. A judge that
#                                   cannot say ❌ is an absent gate (LAWS §5).
# If either control comes back wrong the battery prints `CONTROL FAILED` and exits without a
# verdict, having run no step.
#
# THE RULE FOR A NEW JUDGED STEP. The probe reads the battery's `_step` call sites out of `arroyo`
# itself and requires that EVERY non-empty pattern among them match a line in this file. Add a
# judged step and you add its witness line here, in the section its step belongs to, or the battery
# refuses to run and names your step and your pattern. That is deliberate: a step whose pattern has
# no control line can never be shown to be capable of passing, and a step that cannot pass is the
# defect this fixture exists to make impossible to introduce a second time. Nothing here is
# board-specific and nothing here is a spec directive — `scripts/mbench.py` does not read this file.
#
# PROVENANCE. Every line below is a real spelling lifted from a green capture on this bench
# (the `x86 test-fat sf 300` fold-gate capture of 2026-09-15 and the arm/pi legs beside it), with
# addresses and counters left as they were measured. Keep it short; keep it honest; do not let it
# grow into a second copy of a boot log.

═════════════════════════ CONTROL CAPTURE (synthetic, clean) ═════════════════════════

⚡ kernel features: witness wc

# ── x86 MISSION leg (`x86 test 25`) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
xHCI: >>> MISSION SUCCESS (BOT + CSW). TARGET ACQUIRED. <<<

# ── aarch64 virt MISSION leg (`arm virt v2`) ──────────────────────────────────────────
xHCI: >>> MISSION SUCCESS (BOT + CSW). TARGET ACQUIRED. <<<

# ── usb-write witnesses, x86 and arm (assertions ON the two MISSION captures) ─────────
:: USB: [usbw] write lba=131071 ok — RMW+readback+restore, medium byte-identical ::
:: XHCI: [usbw] write lba=2048 ok — RMW+readback+restore, medium byte-identical ::

# ── x86 test-fat leg (`x86 test-fat sf 300`) ──────────────────────────────────────────
:: U11m2: cross-process unlink-defers-free — open-across-rows + read-after-unlink OK, delete op HELD past unlink, released at last CLOSE and at TEARDOWN, re-create -EBUSY while pending -> PASS ::

# ── the three shipped EL0 apps loaded off the attached FAT volume ─────────────────────
:: WINX-2: STAT.ELF end-to-end — loaded (entry 0x10000000000) + windowed + 3 presents, killed + row reaped by the kill (free 9->10/10, exited 0->0), teardown clean -> PASS ::
:: WINX-8: VUG.ELF end-to-end — loaded (entry 0x100000000b0) + windowed + 3 presents with 2 ring-3 thread(s), killed + row reaped by the kill (free 9->10/10, exited 0->0), teardown clean -> PASS ::
:: PULSE-W: PULSE.ELF end-to-end — loaded (entry 0x10000000000) + windowed + 3 presents of per-core pulse bars sampled through SYS_CPUPULSE(49) -> PASS ::

# ── aarch64 virt v3 capstone leg (`arm virt v3`) ──────────────────────────────────────
:: CAPSTONE COMPLETE — all 6 sync primitives verified in one boot ::

# ── banner-cert's own table, QUOTED here on purpose (BATTFAT) ─────────────────────────
# `scripts/banner-cert.sh` prints one `feature=`/`control=` row per registered witness, and a
# witness TOKEN is verbatim kernel text — two of the control probes carry a verdict spelling INSIDE
# the token. banner-cert runs inside `esp_x86`, whose stdout is the battery's capture, so these rows
# appear in every esp-x86-backed leg's log and they matched the fault scan from BANNERCERT until
# BATTFAT taught the scan to skip a line where the HARNESS is quoting rather than the kernel
# printing. They are in this fixture so that probe (A) exercises that exclusion: delete the
# exclusion from `_step` and probe (A) reds, which is this fixture's go-red.
feature=witness witness=:: U1a: no application processors online — ring-3 demo SKIPPED :: hits=1 -> OK
feature=kbdwit witness=SILENCE-ENDED-HALTED hits=1 -> OK
control=instgui witness=[wc-x] instgui install-go with NO committed target hits=0 -> OK
control=selfhost witness=:: SELFHOST: src payload holds no regular files -> FAIL :: hits=0 -> OK
control=holocron witness=:: [hcron] framing fixture leg=roundtrip -> FAIL hits=0 -> OK
control=videobench witness=:: vperf: fbmem no framebuffer registered :: hits=0 -> OK
control=irqstorage witness=irqstorage::submit called off a scheduled task hits=0 -> OK
⚡ banner-cert: ok=2 missing/leak=0 registered-divergences=0 unverifiable=0 nowitness=0 noverdict=0  (table: 7 measured, 0 unmeasured-here)

═════════════════════════ END OF CONTROL CAPTURE ═════════════════════════
